//? Title: Polynomial Regression: Basis and Bias–Variance
//? Subtitle: A richer polynomial space lowers training error without guaranteeing lower risk
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Curved responses require a richer function class than a straight line, but flexible fits can
amplify observation noise. We construct Legendre features on a tiny quadratic example, then
fit many independent noisy replicas of a known sine function. The finite-ensemble identity
checks bias and variance directly rather than inferring them from one fitted curve.
)
\toc

> The method

Degree-d polynomial regression is linear in d+1 coefficients. Legendre columns span the same
space as powers while improving conditioning on [-1,1]. They satisfy P0=1, P1=x and

$$
P_n(x)=\frac{(2n-1)xP_{n-1}(x)-(n-1)P_{n-2}(x)}n,\qquad
\overline{(\widehat f-f)^2}=(\overline{\widehat f}-f)^2+\overline{(\widehat f-\overline{\widehat f})^2}.
$$
\alttext(Legendre columns follow a three-term recurrence. Ensemble mean squared error equals squared bias plus population variance across fitted replicas.)

The second equality follows by expanding each error around the ensemble mean; the cross term
sums to zero. It is exact for the finite ensemble with divisor R, not R-1. Add the observation
noise variance only when predicting a fresh noisy response rather than the latent mean.
Each fit uses a learnable coefficient tensor and an explicit SGD loop. In the ensemble,
the loss averages over observations and sums over the 100 replica columns, so each
replica receives the same update scale as an independent fit. A single coefficient
matrix trains all replicas concurrently. QR checks the converged coefficients; it does
not perform the demonstrated fit. Each SGD step costs O(N(d+1)R). Extrapolation can
still be unstable even when the in-range training loss is small.

The concept diagram has a retained TikZ source in `diagrams/polynomial_regression_concept.tikz`.
Regeneration requires LaTeX with PGF/TikZ and standalone, and dvisvgm.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/polynomial_regression_concept.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/polynomial_regression_concept.tikz > build/polynomial_regression_concept.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/polynomial_regression_concept.svg build/polynomial_regression_concept.dvi > build/polynomial_regression_concept.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered build/polynomial_regression_concept.svg from local TikZ source.\n'
```
